---
// Lesson 1.2 — The Unity Editor Tour
// The five panes, GameObject/Component/Transform, the real anatomy of the
// Player (Transform + a script with one line of behaviour + a child "Sprite"
// carrying the SpriteRenderer and Animator), and play mode vs edit mode.
//
// STRUCTURE (tightening pass): the pane table's "your move" column WAS the
// editor work, so the five panes are now five numbered steps under "In the
// Editor", each one naming the pane it happens in, with the play-mode demo and
// the save as the last two. "In the Code" is the half a student reads rather
// than clicks: what the Player is made of, and which object carries which
// component. The old "Ship It" activity is gone — commit is a step and a
// checkpoint line now.
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION AND PRE-EXPLANATION: the "Why is the picture on a child
// object?" callout restated the Sprite Renderer row directly above it, and its
// last sentence pre-explained 1.6's GetComponentInChildren search — the one
// lesson where that mistake actually costs the student something. The "One idea
// to carry out of today" paragraph said what the lead and the table's header
// row already say. The play-mode trap stays: it is the second time the vanish
// is explained, and the new context is why Unity was built that way.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the socratic block keeps its
// three slots; the second question is reworded to ask about the play-mode demo
// that is actually on the page.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderPane } from '../../lib/panes';

const body = `<h1>1.2 — The Unity Editor Tour</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Before you write a line of code you need to read the tool that runs everything. Unity is five panes, one Inspector, and a trap called play mode — and it ends with something strange about the Player in this project.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~25 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> left. Every GameObject in this scene, as a tree. Click things: some entries are parents with children indented under them — why?</li>
        <li><strong>Scene view →</strong> centre. The world, editable from above. Scroll to zoom, right-drag to pan, press <strong>F</strong> to focus the selected object.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> what the camera sees. Press <strong>Play</strong> and watch the focus jump to this tab by itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspector →</strong> right. Everything about the selected object, as a list of components. Select the Player and read the whole list.</li>
        <li><strong>Project window →</strong> bottom. The repo's actual files. Navigate to <code>Assets/Scripts</code> and click <code>PlayerController.cs</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong>, select the Player, and set <strong>Inspector → Transform → Position X</strong> to <code>15</code>. The character teleports across the screen. Press <strong>Play</strong> again to stop and it is back where it started, with your <code>15</code> gone.
${renderPane({
    pane: 'Inspector',
    subject: 'Player',
    caption:
        'The Player\'s Inspector. `Transform` is the first component on every object in the game, ' +
        'and `Position` is the first row inside it — the box this step wants is the first of that ' +
        'row\'s three. Your numbers will differ; the row is what matters.',
    rows: [
        { kind: 'component', label: 'Transform', tick: false },
        { kind: 'field', label: 'Position', value: 'X 15    Y 0    Z 0', step: 6 },
        { kind: 'field', label: 'Rotation', value: 'X 0     Y 0    Z 0' },
        { kind: 'field', label: 'Scale', value: 'X 1     Y 1    Z 1' },
        { kind: 'component', label: 'Player Controller (Script)' },
    ],
})}
        </li>
        <li><strong>File → Save</strong> (or <strong>Ctrl+S</strong>) so the work you did keep is written to disk, then commit it in GitHub Desktop.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Play mode is a dream, edit mode is reality.</strong> Everything you change while the Play button is blue — positions, Inspector values, objects dragged into the scene — evaporates the moment you stop. That is the point: you can test an idea without risking the game you have. Experiment in play mode, then stop and enter the winning numbers in edit mode to keep them.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p>One idea to carry out of today: <strong>a GameObject is an empty container, and components are the abilities you bolt onto it.</strong> Here is every component the Player has.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Object</th><th>Component</th><th>What it does here</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Player</strong> <small>(tagged <code>Player</code>)</small></td><td><strong>Transform</strong></td><td>Position, rotation and scale. Every GameObject has one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Player</strong></td><td><strong>Player Controller (Script)</strong></td><td>A nearly empty script. One line in <code>Start()</code> switches the idle animation on; it has no fields and it does not move anything.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sprite</strong> <small>(child of Player)</small></td><td><strong>Transform</strong></td><td>Position <em>relative to its parent</em> — which is why children move when the parent moves.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sprite</strong></td><td><strong>Sprite Renderer</strong></td><td><strong>What you actually see.</strong> The Player root has none, which is why the Player itself is invisible.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sprite</strong></td><td><strong>Animator</strong></td><td>The idle animation that plays when you press Play.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why would Unity be designed so that play-mode changes vanish? Think about testing an idea fifty times a day — what does "reset to the last known good game" buy you?</li>
            <li>The Player carries two components and the Sprite carries three. Which object would you move to move the character, and what would happen to the picture if you moved the child instead?</li>
            <li>Is play mode closer to a save-file system or to an undo button, and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Name all five panes and what each one is for</li>
        <li>Explain "GameObject = container, components = abilities" using the Player as the example</li>
        <li>Point at which object carries the Sprite Renderer and which carries the script</li>
        <li>Demonstrate play-mode vanishing with your own two hands</li>
        <li>Save a scene, then commit and push the change</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — the editor, scripts, input, vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — every API this course uses</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.3.html">1.3 — Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</a>, where the empty script on the Player gets its first real lines.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.2" title="1.2 The Unity Editor Tour — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
